  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday Through Tuesday...\n\nA fairly static 500 mb pattern is expected over the next seven \ndays. High pressure will be generally centered over the Central\nRockies with its fluctuations bringing temperature ranges from\nnear normal to 10 F above normal. Monsoonal flow will also be \npresent as well bringing high PWATs and daily chances for showers \nand thunderstorms. Weak upper level flow will keep shear values \ndown however, acting to limit severe thunderstorm potential. \nHeavy rain is expected to be the main hazard with any \nthunderstorms that develop. \n\nThere is a level 1 of 5 risk for severe thunderstorms Thursday \nfor locations west of Billings. Dewpoints in the 60s F and \nadequate shear of around 30 kts will be enough for isolated severe\nthunderstorms with wind gusts up to 60 mph, hail up to 1 inch, and\nheavy rain being the main threats. There is a risk\nlevel 2 of 5 for severe thunderstorms for Northern Park County \n(including Livingston) due to an increased risk for hail. Heavy \nrain will bring an increased risk (level 1 of 4) for flash \nflooding. Showers and thunderstorms are expected to begin around \n3 PM and last through the evening. \n\nFriday through the weekend sees the upper level ridge move north \nbringing warmer temperatures in the 90s F to low 100s F. This \nwill also act to limit shear while much above average moisture is \nstill present. Saturated and slow moving thunderstorms bring the \npotential for heavy rain, especially for locations west of Rosebud\nCounty. This pattern is expected to continue into next week with\nsome potential slight variations such as cooler temperatures,\nthough this is uncertain at this time. Torgerson",
